WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  justice delayed is no longer justice  denied for the passengers and crew  killed 32 years ago this week  aboard pan am flight 103. the london to  new york boeing 747  exploded over lockerbie scotland shortly  after takeoff  all 259 people on the plane  including 190 americans were killed  as were 11 people on the ground this  week  charges against the alleged libyan bomb  maker were unsealed  and one of the most complex terrorism  cases in u.s  history catherine heritage has the story  the quiet night in a scottish village  shattered  by a new york bound pan-american jumbo  jet falling in flames from the sky  all 258 people on board killed four days  before  christmas in 1988 the shattered nose and  cockpit  bearing the name clipper made of the  seas symbolized  american lives lost to international  terrorism  my brother rick minnetti was one of  passengers on the plane outside the  justice department this week  cara weeps told us rick was a junior at  syracuse  university studying abroad in the uk  he was going to live his life to the  fullest in the weeks that he had while  he was in london  along with rick 34 classmates from  syracuse university died  when the bomb ripped through the luggage  hold an hour into the flight  from london to new york's jfk 259 lives  cut  short on the flight 11 more killed by  falling debris in lockerbie scotland  the families have fought very hard over  the last 32 years  to make sure that this was an open  investigation and remained an open  investigation  the pan am 103 case has come full circle  for the outgoing attorney general bill  barr  who led the justice department when  initial charges were made in 1991  we will not rest until all those  responsible  are brought to justice at the justice  department 29 years later  barr announced charges against the  alleged bomb maker  no amount of time or distance will stop  the united states  and our scottish partners from pursuing  justice  according to newly unsealed court  records the bomb maker  abu aguilar mohammed masood confessed in  2012  and carried out the attacks on behalf of  the late libyan dictator muammar gaddafi  the pan am flight exploded at 30  thousand feet  u.s attorney michael sherwin it took  literally four to five minutes for the  debris to hit the ground  it fell into a debris scatter pattern of  over 840 square miles  which was and i believe still remains  today the largest crime scene in world  history  like a giant jigsaw puzzle an  international team of investigators  pieced together the jet ultimately  leading to fragments of the cassette  recorder  packed with explosives it was a copper  piece of samsonite luggage that was only  sold in the middle east at that time  embedded within some of those tiny  pieces of clothing were tiny  pieces of black plastic that  forensically were linked to a toshiba  radio cassette  is there evidence that other suspects  remain out there  from what we understand yes how many  we don't know but it doesn't end with  the bomb maker no  cara still takes inspiration from her  late brother rick  i posted a journal entry that we  got back from the remains that they  found  in lockerbie i think the quote is do all  you can while you can life is a one-time  deal  for cbs this morning saturday katherine  herridge washington  wow for him to be on that case bar at  the beginning and now at the end saying  no amount of time or distance will stop  the united states from pursuing justice  in this case  yeah and for these families it's just  such an agonizing time of year this must  be and especially now with this news  reliving it must be so incredibly  difficult hopefully they get justice  without a doubt

